 "title": "Threads: start() makes one, run() does not",
 "kind": "computer",
 "intro": "A Thread object is just an object until start() is called. Which thread prints when we call run() instead?",
 "predict": {
  "question": "main calls t.run() on a Thread whose task prints the current thread's name. What prints?",
  "options": [
   "hi from Thread-0",
   "hi from main",
   "Both names, on two cores"
  ],
  "answer": 1,
  "reveal": "run() is a normal method call, so main itself executes it. Only start() asks the JVM for a new thread with its own stack."
 },
 "takeaway": "start() creates a new thread that runs run() on its own stack; calling run() directly is just a method call on the current thread.",
